WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.127
NET provides cryptographic APIs through System.

2
00:00:03.127 --> 00:00:04.457
Security.

3
00:00:04.457 --> 00:00:05.787
Cryptography.

4
00:00:05.787 --> 00:00:17.761
Those APIs include support for hashing, keyed message authentication, random number generation, symmetric encryption, asymmetric cryptography, signatures, and certificate handling.

5
00:00:17.761 --> 00:00:24.280
They are powerful, but power does not remove the need to understand purpose and usage constraints.

6
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:25.611
ASP.

7
00:00:25.611 --> 00:00:30.533
NET Core also provides higher-level services for common application needs.

8
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:36.055
Data Protection is used for framework and application payload protection scenarios.

9
00:00:36.055 --> 00:00:37.385
ASP.

10
00:00:37.385 --> 00:00:43.971
NET Core Identity provides maintained password hashing behavior for password-based sign-in systems.

11
00:00:43.971 --> 00:00:50.690
These higher-level services are usually safer than building a new scheme around low-level primitives.

12
00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:56.410
TLS, certificates, platform trust stores, and hosting configuration are part of many .

13
00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:57.807
NET security designs.

14
00:00:57.807 --> 00:01:11.245
For web applications and services, cryptography is often provided through the transport layer, identity provider, hosting platform, gateway, or managed service rather than hand-written application code.

15
00:01:11.245 --> 00:01:20.092
Cloud key stores, HSM-backed services, and managed identity can support key protection and access control in production environments.

16
00:01:20.092 --> 00:01:26.611
They can reduce direct exposure of key material, centralize permissions, and improve auditability.

17
00:01:26.611 --> 00:01:32.931
They still need governance, permission review, lifecycle management, and operational ownership.

18
00:01:32.931 --> 00:01:38.519
Developers should prefer high-level, maintained, purpose-built APIs where available.

19
00:01:38.519 --> 00:01:45.769
Low-level primitives should be used only when the protocol and requirements are well understood and reviewed.

20
00:01:45.769 --> 00:01:52.621
A low-level API can be correct in isolation while the surrounding construction is unsafe or incomplete.

21
00:01:52.621 --> 00:01:53.952
The practical .

22
00:01:53.952 --> 00:02:00.138
NET toolbox decision is to choose the highest-level maintained service that fits the purpose.

23
00:02:00.138 --> 00:02:18.099
Use Identity password hashing for passwords, Data Protection for framework-style protected payloads, TLS for transport, certificate validation for service identity, and reviewed cryptography APIs only when the application genuinely needs direct cryptographic operations.
